% Absent under negation, in the §16.8 sparse-table shape: a `food` row whose id
% is missing, and a `measurement` row whose food key is missing too. This is the
% case that discriminates the two notions of "same" (§4) at the anti-join — the
% no-match pattern closes a slot to `absent`, and whether a stored `absent` cell
% refutes it is exactly the question. See spec §7 and §17 (2026-07-29).
food(absent).
food("apple").
food("kale").

measurement(absent, 3).
measurement("apple", 5).

% "things with no measurement" — the query the change is measured on.
unmeasured(F) :- food(F), not measurement(F, _).

% Non-contradiction: no body can be satisfied by both `p(X)` and `not p(X)`.
contradiction(F) :- food(F), not food(F).

?- unmeasured(F).
?- contradiction(F).
